  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Podium Night",
    "overview": "An awards night run from a laptop, voted on from phones and revealed on whatever screen the room has. The fun lives in how the results arrive, in the timing and the words, and in a light touch everywhere else: rounded, friendly and clear from across the room, polished the way Nintendo, NYT Games or a Kahoot round is. It is built to be fun on the ninth award of the night, not just the first, so nothing is a gimmick that wears thin: no costume theme, no novelty textures, no mascot.\n\nOne system at three volumes. The big screen is loudest: display type, the podium race and confetti. The voter's phone is medium: big tap targets and one moment of delight when a vote is in. Host views are quiet: the same colours and type, with nothing playful in the controls. The show screens carry no AwardBro branding; they belong to the people in the room.\n\nThere are two themes from one set of roles. **Night** (dark) is the default, because most shows happen in the evening in a dim room. **Paper** (light) is the same system on warm paper for daytime rooms and bright projectors. The big screen's theme is set by the host per show (Night by default, Paper on the manage page's Night/Paper switch), and the screen follows it live; phones and host views follow the device setting, with an override. A page puts its theme on `<html>` (`data-theme=\"light\"` for Paper, nothing for Night), so dialogs, tooltips and toasts, which render outside the page, follow it too.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Rounded everything: Fredoka type, pill buttons, rounded-top podium columns.",
      "Solid, saturated colour that survives a washed-out projector; no glows or gradients.",
      "Results as a podium race: columns rise, each nomination in its own colour, the leader stalls, the winner turns gold and lands.",
      "A small shape set (circle, triangle, squiggle) as markers and confetti, nothing more.",
      "Soft coloured shadows; never hard offset shadows."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Gold Is Earned Rule",
        "body": "The winner colour belongs to the winner alone: never a button, never a runner-up's column. Nothing else on the reveal is gold (or Podium Coral on Paper), so the moment it appears, the room knows.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Seeded Colour Rule",
        "body": "Column colours look random but aren't: they are shuffled with the award as the seed, so the big screen, the host's laptop and every reload show the same colours. Up to seven nominations never repeat a colour; beyond seven, colours repeat but never side by side.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Projector Rule",
        "body": "Colour is solid and saturated. No tints below 20% contrast, no gradients and no glow: a cheap projector washes out the subtle and keeps the bold.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Stage Unit Rule",
        "body": "Everything on the big screen is sized in stage units: type from the `text-stage-*` scale, spacing from Tailwind's scale (which counts in half-units there), anything else as a multiple of `u`. Never a fixed pixel or rem size on the big screen, or it breaks on the next screen size.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Kicker Rule",
        "body": "No small uppercase label above a heading. Context such as \"Award 4 of 9\" sits beside the progress dots; \"And the winner is…\" is a timed beat of its own, not a label.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Overscan Rule",
        "body": "Some TVs crop the outer 5% of the picture, so nothing that has to be read sits there: the margins clear it, and a column can run off the bottom edge only because its count and name sit at its top, above the floor (24u).",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Hard Offset Rule",
        "body": "Never a solid offset shadow (the sticker or Memphis look). Shadows blur.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Duotone Moment Rule",
        "body": "Bold for controls; Duotone (`weight=\"duotone\"`) only for the few expressive moments: the category-type badges, a voter's vote confirmation and empty states. Its second layer is the text colour at 20%, so it works in both themes without a token of its own. Never Duotone on a button.",
        "section": "icons"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep the action colour for actions and the winner colour for the winner alone.",
      "**Do** write copy that says what happens next (\"Watch the big screen for the results.\").",
      "**Do** respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: show the end state at once.",
      "**Do** check every text pair against both themes; body text stays at 4.5:1 or more.",
      "**Do** size everything on the big screen in stage units, and keep what must be read clear of the outer 5%."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** put AwardBro branding on the big screen or the vote page.",
      "**Don't** use hard offset shadows, gradients or glows.",
      "**Don't** add themed decoration (envelopes, stamps, tickets, textures): it gets old by the third award.",
      "**Don't** put a small uppercase label above a heading."
    ]
  }
}
